## 3. CRUD round-trips through the store + validation errors surfaced — PASS

Live server: binary built to scratch, BUDGET_DATA_DIR pointed at a scratch
fixture dir holding usaa-checking.csv, usaa-credit-2026-08.csv,
vanguard-2026.csv. Listening 127.0.0.1:18641.

CREATE — POST /accounts (id=zz-check, name="ZZ Checking", kind=checking,
file_patterns="usaa-check*.csv", low_balance_threshold=750) → 200. The sidecar
written by accounts.Save, read back off disk:

    {"accounts":[{"id":"zz-check","name":"ZZ Checking","institution":"USAA",
      "kind":"checking","file_patterns":["usaa-check*.csv"],
      "low_balance_threshold":750,"created_at":"2026-08-17T13:05:28...","...

EDIT — POST /accounts/aa-all (name="AA Renamed", kind=savings,
low_balance_threshold=1200) → 200; response HTML contains "AA Renamed"; disk:

    [('zz-check','ZZ Checking','checking',750,0),
     ('aa-all','AA Renamed','savings',1200,1)]

Note the trailing 1: the pre-existing anchor survived the edit — the edit path
merges fields rather than replacing the record (handlers.go:431-442), so anchor
data is not silently destroyed by a name change.

DELETE — see item 7. Persisted removal confirmed on disk.

VALIDATION, duplicate ID — POST /accounts with id=aa-all (already exists) → 200,
body carries the error verbatim from accounts.Validate (accounts.go:139):

    duplicate account ID &#34;aa-all&#34;

with `role="alert"` present (1 occurrence) and `aria-invalid="true"` on the
offending field. Disk after: ['zz-check','aa-all'] — the second record was NOT
written. This is genuinely routed through accounts.Save: handlers.go:443-454
ALWAYS appends on create (never upserts), specifically so Validate sees the
duplicate rather than the handler silently overwriting.

VALIDATION, empty name — POST /accounts (id=no-name, name="   ") → 200, body:

    account name is required

Disk unchanged: ['zz-check','aa-all'].

Judgment call recorded, not a defect: the empty-name error is raised by the
handler's own pre-check (handlers.go:419-422) before accounts.Save is reached,
so accounts.Validate's own empty-name string is never the one displayed. The
user-visible outcome is identical (error shown, nothing persisted), and the
brief's requirement is "surfaced to the user rather than swallowed", which
holds. Same for empty ID.

## 4. Pattern editor resolves against the data directory — PASS (strongest evidence)

This is the item most at risk of being satisfied cosmetically, so I proved it
three ways against the live server, all of which would fail if the page merely
echoed the typed patterns.

(a) GLOB RESOLUTION. The account was created with the pattern
`usaa-check*.csv`. The rendered "Matched files" list does not contain that
string; it contains the resolved filename:

    <h4 ...>Matched files</h4>
    <ul ...><li class="font-mono">usaa-checking.csv</li></ul>

and the same file simultaneously disappeared from the Unassigned section, which
before the create had listed all three fixtures:

    before: usaa-checking.csv, usaa-credit-2026-08.csv, vanguard-2026.csv
    after:  usaa-credit-2026-08.csv, vanguard-2026.csv

An echo of the typed text cannot produce a different string, and cannot move a
file between two sections.

(b) FIRST MATCH WINS BY ASCENDING ACCOUNT ID. I then created a second account
`aa-all` whose ID sorts BEFORE `zz-check` and whose pattern is the bare
substring `usaa`. Live response:

    === AA Catchall ===  Matched files
      <li>usaa-checking.csv</li><li>usaa-credit-2026-08.csv</li>
    === ZZ Checking ===  Matched files
      "No CSV files in the data directory currently match this account's patterns."

The catch-all took usaa-checking.csv away from the account holding the MORE
SPECIFIC glob, purely because its ID sorts first. That is exactly
accounts.MatchFile's documented contract (accounts.go:179-189, sortedByID at
:234-239) and is the opposite of what a naive most-specific-wins or
per-account-independent implementation would render. handlers.go:486-489 does
in fact call accounts.MatchFile once per basename and then buckets by winner
(:494-506), so the page and the loader cannot disagree.

(c) SUBSTRING FALLBACK + UNASSIGNED. `usaa` (not a glob) matched both usaa
files via accountMatches' substring fallback (accounts.go:225), and
vanguard-2026.csv — matching nothing — remained listed under
`id="accounts-unassigned-heading"` with the explanatory text "These CSV files
match no account's patterns. They still load, but their rows carry no account
attribution."

The file list itself comes from a real glob of the data directory
(handlers.go:565-585, filepath.Glob(dir+"/*.csv")), the same glob
accounts.existingCSVBasenames uses (accounts.go:245-256).

